      "sequence_position": 13,
      "problem": "Prove that if $n$ is an even integer, then ${n}^{2}+4$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=\\,${{field-1}} for some integer $a.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+4$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+4 & ={{field-2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet $b=$ {{field-3}}. Since $a$ is {{field-4}}, we have that $b$ is {{field-5}}.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{n}^{2}+4={{field-6}},\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ Therefore, ${n}^{2}+4$ is {{field-7}}.",
      "worked_solution": "Recall the following:\n\n- If an integer $n$ is even, then there exists an integer $p$ such that $n=2p.$\n\n- If an integer $n$ is odd, then there exists an integer $q$ such that $n=2q+1.$\n\nIn this case, we need to prove the following:\n\n$$\nn\\text{ is even}\\,⇒\\,{n}^{2}+4\\text{ is even}\n$$\n\nWe start by using the fact that $n$ is even.\n\nIf $n$ is even, it can be expressed as $n=2a$ for some integer $a.$ \n\nThe idea is to substitute $n=2a$ into our expression ${n}^{2}+4$ and show it is even by writing it as a multiple of $2.$\n\nNow, writing ${n}^{2}+4$ in terms of $a,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+4 & =(2a)^{2}+4 \\\\  & =4{a}^{2}+4 \\\\  & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2)}_{⏟}}_{b}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, if we define another integer $b=2{a}^{2}+2a$ (highlighted above), we have that ${n}^{2}+4=2b,$ which shows that ${n}^{2}+4$ is indeed a multiple of $2.$\n\nLet $b=2{a}^{2}+2.$ Since $a$ is an integer, we have that $b$ is an integer.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{n}^{2}+4 & =2{{(2{a}^{2}+2)}_{⏟}}_{b} \\\\  & =2b\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich is a multiple of $2.$ \n\nFinally, we write our conclusion.\n\nTherefore, ${n}^{2}+4$ is even.",
